// The look-dev day (M1 X1 part 2): the time of day `--look-dev` runs on instead of the default
// day, so the default day and every scene that isn't the look-dev town stay as they are. The
// sandbox compiles it in (`apps/sapphire_sandbox/src/lookdev.rs`); it is a `TodTimeline` document
// (`sapphire_world::tod`, whose module docs say how keys blend), and hot reload watches it in dev
// builds while `--look-dev` runs. The cooker leaves it alone: no importer reads a bare `.ron`.
//
// The target is Wuthering Waves' light (the user's reference set, described in docs/m1-plan.md's
// X1 row): a strong warm sun with clearly cool, lifted shadows by day; a huge warm sun glow, warm
// cloud sides over purple undersides and a peach-to-violet sky at golden hour; a cyan-blue blue
// hour with the windows coming on; and a blue night, not a black one, with a navy sky, a teal
// Milky Way and moonlit lavender clouds, mid-tones kept readable by exposure.
//
// - Ten keys: midnight, the blue hour before dawn (4:45), sunrise (6:00), morning (9:00), midday
//   (13:00, `lookdev-town-day`), afternoon (16:00), golden hour (18:00, `lookdev-town-hero`;
//   `lookdev-town-golden` runs at 18:30), sunset (19:00), the blue hour after it (19:45,
//   `lookdev-town-blue`) and nightfall (21:00; `lookdev-town-night` runs at 23:00). Each sets
//   every group.
// - The sun, as the stage's cameras see it (they look toward -Z from the square's open side):
//   it rises on the right behind the cameras, stands on the right at about 37 degrees at midday
//   (side light: the facades facing the cameras half lit, long shadows across the square), swings
//   round behind them to the left in the afternoon, and sets in front of them on the left. At
//   golden hour it is a low sun at the wide shot's top left, about 27 degrees up: its glow fills
//   that corner and long warm light crosses the square between blue-violet shadows; by 18:30 only
//   warm streaks through the gaps at the left row's end remain. The moon rises on the right
//   behind the cameras at dusk, stands high behind them at midnight (moonlit facades) and sets on
//   the left before dawn. Directions are the way light travels, from the body into the scene.
// - Aerial perspective in layers: near haze starts 25–45 m out, so the square and the character
//   stay clear; far haze from 45–70 m takes the sky's horizon color (white tint), so the hills
//   lift and the mountains' feet melt into it; the height layer is thin and deep (falloff 0.01–
//   0.015 a meter), light blue-grey by day, blue-violet at golden hour, cyan-blue at the blue
//   hour and navy at night, and is the valley's haze that fades the mountains whole, their peaks
//   least. The sky takes the same fog at infinite distance, so the zeniths are authored a little
//   deeper than they show. The key light's forward scatter warms the haze around the sun,
//   strongly at golden hour.
// - Shadow tints multiply the key light's color, so the warm keys' tints lean further toward blue
//   than they look: (0.12, 0.2, 0.7) under the golden sun reads as a clear blue-violet shadow,
//   where a lavender tint would turn the shadows pink.
// - Characters get a key of their own that follows the world's (CLAUDE.md's night character
//   light contract, held by a test in `lookdev.rs` at every quarter hour): the sun's own light
//   from morning to afternoon; a little warmer at golden hour; between 1.2 and 1.6 times the key
//   light's luminance from sunset through the night, warm enough that skin stays warm against the
//   blue world. Exposed, that keeps them a step above the world at night and under 70% of their
//   noon brightness.
// - Post, at every key: wide soft bloom (scatter 0.75–0.8) that opens up at night for the
//   lanterns and windows; the grade lifts the shadows toward blue and keeps a slight warm gain
//   on the highlights (a split tone), with saturation a little up; a mild vignette, tinted at
//   golden hour and night; the diffusion glow clean by day, gold at golden hour, cool at night;
//   the three gradient layers keep their shapes and modes all day, so they blend smoothly. The
//   lens flare is off from golden hour on: its ghosts cut across the hero shot.
// - Every number is a starting point for art direction, not a measurement.
(
    type: "sapphire::world::TodTimeline",
    version: 1,
    data: (
        keys: [
            (
                // Midnight: a blue night under a high moon behind the cameras, lifted by exposure; a navy
